1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,518
OK, Greg,

2
00:00:03,518 --> 00:00:07,800
so I finally managed to read the article you mentioned

3
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:10,890
- the one about the study on gender in physics.

4
00:00:10,890 --> 00:00:15,820
About the study of college students done by Akira Miyake and his team?

5
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:16,705
Yeah.

6
00:00:16,705 --> 00:00:23,020
I was interested that the researchers were actually a mix of psychologists and physicists.

7
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:25,840
That's an unusual combination.Yeah.

8
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:31,627
I got a little confused at first about which students the study was based

9
00:00:31,627 --> 00:00:32,040
on.

10
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:37,468
They weren't actually majoring in physics - they were majoring in what's known as

11
00:00:37,468 --> 00:00:38,720
the STEM disciplines.

12
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:42,349
That's science, technology, engineering and ......

13
00:00:42,349 --> 00:00:43,800
and math.

14
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:43,850
Yes,

15
00:00:43,850 --> 00:00:48,670
but they were all doing physics courses as part of their studies.

16
00:00:48,670 --> 00:00:49,860
That's correct.

17
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:54,620
So as I understood it, Miyake and co

18
00:00:54,620 --> 00:01:01,840
started from the fact that women are underrepresented in introductory physics courses at college,

19
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:07,433
and also that on average, the women who do enrol on these courses perform

20
00:01:07,433 --> 00:01:09,430
more poorly than the men.

21
00:01:09,430 --> 00:01:12,360
No one really knows why this is the case.

22
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:12,764
Yeah.

23
00:01:12,764 --> 00:01:16,000
But what the researchers wanted to find out

24
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,853
was basically what they could do about the relatively low level of the women's

25
00:01:20,853 --> 00:01:21,200
results.

26
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:25,496
But in order to find a solution they needed to find out more about

27
00:01:25,496 --> 00:01:27,030
the nature of the problem.

28
00:01:27,030 --> 00:01:30,107
Right - now let's see if I can remember...

29
00:01:30,107 --> 00:01:32,800
it was that in the physics class,

30
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:39,262
the female students thought the male students all assumed that women weren't any good

31
00:01:39,262 --> 00:01:40,185
at physics ...

32
00:01:40,185 --> 00:01:41,570
was that it?

33
00:01:41,570 --> 00:01:47,049
And they thought that the men expected them to get poor results in their

34
00:01:47,049 --> 00:01:47,440
tests.

35
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:49,193
That's what the women thought,

36
00:01:49,193 --> 00:01:50,946
and that made them nervous,

37
00:01:50,946 --> 00:01:53,050
so they did get poor results.

38
00:01:53,050 --> 00:01:55,112
But actually they were wrong ...

39
00:01:55,112 --> 00:02:00,060
No one was making any assumptions about the female students at all.

40
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:04,150
Anyway, what Miyake's team did was quite simple -

41
00:02:04,150 --> 00:02:08,900
getting the students to do some writing before they went into the physics class.

42
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:10,630
What did they call it?

43
00:02:10,630 --> 00:02:16,237
Values-affirmation - they had to write an essay focusing on things that were significant

44
00:02:16,237 --> 00:02:17,100
to them,

45
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:21,253
not particularly to do with the subject they were studying,

46
00:02:21,253 --> 00:02:23,330
but more general things like

47
00:02:23,330 --> 00:02:23,694
music,

48
00:02:23,694 --> 00:02:25,880
or people who mattered to them.

49
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:26,265
Right.

50
00:02:26,265 --> 00:02:31,659
So the idea of doing the writing is that this gets the students thinking

51
00:02:31,659 --> 00:02:33,200
in a positive way.

52
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:38,635
And putting these thoughts into words can relax them and help them overcome the

53
00:02:38,635 --> 00:02:41,740
psychological factors that lead to poor performance.Yeah.

54
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:45,580
But what the researchers in the study hadn't expected

55
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:51,187
was that this one activity raised the women's physics grades from the C to

56
00:02:51,187 --> 00:02:53,590
the B range.A huge change.

57
00:02:53,590 --> 00:02:55,772
Pity it wasn't to an A,

58
00:02:55,772 --> 00:02:56,500
but still!

59
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:56,983
No,

60
00:02:56,983 --> 00:03:03,750
but it does suggest that the women were seriously underperforming beforehand, in comparison with

61
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:05,200
the men.Yes.

62
00:03:05,200 --> 00:03:09,500
Mind you, Miyake's article left out a lot of details.

63
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:13,078
Like, did the students do the writing just once,

64
00:03:13,078 --> 00:03:14,270
or several times?

65
00:03:14,270 --> 00:03:17,860
And had they been told why they were doing the writing?

66
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:19,920
That might have affected the results.

67
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:24,992
You mean, if they know the researchers thought it might help them to improve,

68
00:03:24,992 --> 00:03:27,890
then they'd just try to fulfil that expectation?

69
00:03:27,890 --> 00:03:28,820
Exactly.

70
00:03:28,820 --> 00:03:37,430
So anyway, I thought for our project we could do a similar study,

71
00:03:37,430 --> 00:03:42,530
but investigate whether it really was the writing activity that had that result.

72
00:03:42,530 --> 00:03:43,033
Ok.

73
00:03:43,033 --> 00:03:50,080
So we could ask them to do a writing task about something completely different...

74
00:03:50,080 --> 00:03:51,644
something more factual?

75
00:03:51,644 --> 00:03:54,250
Like a general knowledge topic.

76
00:03:54,250 --> 00:03:54,714
Maybe...

77
00:03:54,714 --> 00:03:59,821
or we could have half the students doing a writing task

78
00:03:59,821 --> 00:04:04,000
and half doing something else, like an oral task.

79
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,310
Or even, half do the same writing task as in the original research

80
00:04:09,310 --> 00:04:12,170
and half do a factual writing task.

81
00:04:12,170 --> 00:04:16,679
Then we'd see if it really is the topic that made the difference,

82
00:04:16,679 --> 00:04:17,720
or something else.

83
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:18,475
That's it.

84
00:04:18,475 --> 00:04:18,853
Good.

85
00:04:18,853 --> 00:04:24,140
So at our meeting with the supervisor on Monday we can tell him we've

86
00:04:24,140 --> 00:04:25,650
decided on our project.

87
00:04:25,650 --> 00:04:28,762
We should have our aims ready by them.

88
00:04:28,762 --> 00:04:33,820
I suppose we need to read the original study-the article's just a summary.

89
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:37,391
And there was another article I read, by Smolinsky.

90
00:04:37,391 --> 00:04:42,946
It was about her research on how women and men perform in mixed teams

91
00:04:42,946 --> 00:04:43,740
in class,

92
00:04:43,740 --> 00:04:47,100
compared with single-sex teams and on their own.

93
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:51,680
Let me guess...the women were better at teamwork.That's what I expected,

94
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:57,492
but actually the men and the women got the same results whether they were working in teams

95
00:04:57,492 --> 00:04:58,860
or on their own.

96
00:04:58,860 --> 00:05:02,440
But I guess it's not that relevant to us.

97
00:05:02,440 --> 00:05:07,527
What worries me anyway is how we're going to get everything done in the

98
00:05:07,527 --> 00:05:07,890
time.

99
00:05:07,890 --> 00:05:10,310
We'll be OK now what we are doing.

100
00:05:10,310 --> 00:05:18,206
Though I'm not clear how we assess whether the students in our experiment actually make any progress

101
00:05:18,206 --> 00:05:19,600
or not...No.

102
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:22,047
We may need some advice on that.

103
00:05:22,047 --> 00:05:27,291
The main thing's to make sure we have the right size sample, not too big

104
00:05:27,291 --> 00:05:28,340
or too small.

105
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:30,027
That's shouldn't be difficult.

106
00:05:30,027 --> 00:05:33,400
Right, what do we need to do next?

107
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:36,820
We could have a look at the timetable for the science classes...

108
00:05:36,820 --> 00:05:41,588
or perhaps we should just make an appointment to see one of the science

109
00:05:41,588 --> 00:05:41,928
professors.

110
00:05:41,928 --> 00:05:42,950
That's be better.

111
00:05:42,950 --> 00:05:43,274
Great.

112
00:05:43,274 --> 00:05:46,840
And we could even got to observe one of the class.

113
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:47,790
What for?

114
00:05:47,790 --> 00:05:54,098
Well...OK maybe let's just go with your idea.

115
00:05:54,098 --> 00:05:55,500
Right, well...
